{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>You shall not bow down, etc.</b> One must know why it was necessary to command this after He had already issued several warnings against this perversion and its like — all the more so since just above He warned even against letting their names be mentioned on one's lips, and against causing their names to be recalled, as it is written (Exodus 23:13): ״וְלֹא יִשָּׁמַע עַל פִּיךָ״. If so, what place is there to warn further concerning idolatrous worship? And the Ramban will answer that because it is so severe, He multiplied warnings concerning it. Yet it is astonishing, for after He equated it to the entire Torah as stated above — both regarding punishment and regarding reward — behold, one who worships it transgresses 613 prohibitions; all the more so given that there are commandments that contain two or more negative prohibitions, and this commandment is weighed against them all." "<b>And it appears</b> to say that the reason of the verse is for three causes: The first — since He wished to warn that one should not imitate the nations in their deeds, therefore, had He not prefaced by saying ״לֹא תִשְׁתַּחֲוֶה וְלֹא תָעָבְדֵם,״ it would not occur to human mind to say that the verse intends to speak of a profane act that contains no idolatry yet is forbidden; and necessarily one must say that it speaks only of an act of prohibition, which is idolatry. But after He said <b>you shall not bow down to them and you shall not serve them</b>, by this He revealed His intent that what He said — <b>you shall not do like their deeds</b> — applies even to something in which it is not recognizable that there is idolatry, namely that one should not imitate them neither in their dress nor in their tonsure at all; and included here is everything that is uniquely designated for the practice of the nations, as stated in the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Shabbat 67a). The second — to inform us that all their deeds contain idolatry, for HaShem knows the thoughts of human beings and the foundation of everything; and if so, whoever performs a deed of the nations tastes of the sin of idolatry. The third — since He wished to command concerning their demolition and destruction, therefore He prefaced by saying <b>you shall not bow down</b>, etc., to say that if one did not demolish and did not break them, he is tantamount to one who worships idolatry, for HaShem holds accountable for the thought in idolatry, and they need an active expression of negating the thought of idolatry from their heart, and there is no expression other than by breaking their monuments; and if they do not do so, they are tantamount to worshippers of idolatry. And this is your sign. Therefore it was necessary to say <b>you shall not bow down to them and you shall not serve them, etc. — but you shall utterly demolish them</b>, to say that in the absence of demolition he transgresses ״לֹא תָעָבְדֵם.״ And you will find that HaShem was emphatic with Israel that they did not do so upon their entry into the land (Judges 2:2)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:09.176103579Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}